AGME project plan
Bhai, ye lo ek bahot detailed, professional aur badi README.md file ka code. Isme architecture, features, database schema, API endpoints, installation steps, aur interview explanation—sab kuch hai.

Tum is code ko copy karke apne README.md file mein paste kar do. GitHub pe ekdum premium aur enterprise-level dikhega (chahe project practice level ka ho).

👇 README.md Ka Code (Copy below text)
markdown
<div align="center">

# 🧠 AGME — Artificial General Memory Engine

**Give your AI Agents a Real Brain. Not Just a Context Window.**

[![FastAPI](https://img.shields.io/badge/Backend-FastAPI-009688?style=for-the-badge&logo=fastapi)](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/)
[![React](https://img.shields.io/badge/Frontend-React-61DAFB?style=for-the-badge&logo=react)](https://reactjs.org/)
[![Gemini API](https://img.shields.io/badge/AI-Gemini%20API-4285F4?style=for-the-badge&logo=google)](https://aistudio.google.com/)
[![SQLite](https://img.shields.io/badge/Database-SQLite-003B57?style=for-the-badge&logo=sqlite)](https://www.sqlite.org/)
[![ChromaDB](https://img.shields.io/badge/Vector%20DB-ChromaDB-FF6B6B?style=for-the-badge)](https://www.trychroma.com/)

**A CPU-friendly, biologically-inspired memory engine for AI agents.**  
*Episodic • Semantic • Procedural • Working Memory*  
*Built for practice. Designed to impress.*

</div>

---

## 📖 Table of Contents
1. [The Problem](#-the-problem)
2. [The Solution (AGME)](#-the-solution-agme)
3. [The 4 Memory Types](#-the-4-memory-types)
4. [System Architecture](#-system-architecture)
5. [Core Features](#-core-features)
6. [Tech Stack](#-tech-stack)
7. [Database Schema](#-database-schema)
8. [API Endpoints](#-api-endpoints)
9. [Memory Lifecycle (How it works)](#-memory-lifecycle-how-it-works)
10. [Installation & Setup](#-installation--setup)
11. [Project Folder Structure](#-project-folder-structure)
12. [Demo Flow](#-demo-flow)
13. [Interview Preparation (Design Decisions)](#-interview-preparation-design-decisions)
14. [Future Scope](#-future-scope)

---

## ❌ The Problem

Modern AI agents (ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude) suffer from **context amnesia**. 
Once you close the chat window, the AI forgets everything about you. Every new session starts from absolute zero. Why? Because LLMs only have a temporary **Context Window**, not a permanent memory system.

Current solutions like massive context windows or basic RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) are flawed:
- **Big Context Windows:** Expensive, slow, and still temporary.
- **Basic Vector Search:** Flat, unorganized, and cannot "forget" irrelevant data.

---

## ✅ The Solution (AGME)

**AGME (Artificial General Memory Engine)** is a middleware memory layer that sits between the user and the LLM. 
It mimics **human cognitive psychology** by implementing 4 distinct types of memory. It automatically extracts facts from conversations, organizes them, consolidates them over time, and intelligently **forgets** irrelevant information.

**Why "CPU-Friendly"?**
Most AI memory projects require heavy GPUs to run local models (like Llama-3 or Sentence-BERT). AGME offloads all heavy AI computation to the **Gemini API (Cloud)**. Your local machine only runs lightweight orchestration (FastAPI, SQLite, ChromaDB). This means the entire system runs smoothly on an average 8GB RAM laptop with **zero GPU requirements**.

---

## 🧩 The 4 Memory Types

Inspired by cognitive psychology (Atkinson-Shiffrin model), AGME classifies memories just like a human brain:

| Memory Type | What it stores | Example | Lifetime | Decay Speed |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 🎬 **Episodic** | Time-stamped events & experiences | *"On Monday, user asked to debug a Python error."* | Medium | Fast |
| 📚 **Semantic** | General facts & concepts (Timeless) | *"User's name is Rahul. User is learning Python."* | Long | Slow |
| 🛠️ **Procedural** | How-to knowledge & skills | *"User prefers short answers. Use 'pdb' for debugging."* | Very Long | Very Slow |
| ⚡ **Working** | Immediate short-term context | *"Current topic: React hooks."* | Seconds/Mins | Instant |

---

## 🏗️ System Architecture

```text
┌─────────────────┐
│   React UI      │ (Frontend Dashboard & Chat)
│  (Vite+Tailwind)│
└────────┬────────┘
         │ REST API (Axios)
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐      ┌──────────────────────┐
│  FastAPI Backend│◄────►│  Gemini API (Cloud)  │
│  (Python 3.10+) │      │  - LLM (gemini-flash)│
└────────┬────────┘      │  - Embeddings        │
         │               └──────────────────────┘
         ├─────────────────────────┐
         ▼                         ▼
┌─────────────────┐      ┌──────────────────────┐
│    SQLite DB    │      │      ChromaDB        │
│ (SQLAlchemy ORM)│      │ (Vector Store for    │
│ - Users         │      │  Semantic Search)    │
│ - Memories      │      └──────────────────────┘
│ - Logs          │
└─────────────────┘
✨ Core Features
Autonomous Extraction: LLM reads the chat and automatically writes memories (no manual tagging).

Importance Scoring: Each memory gets a score from 1-10. (Name = 9/10, random joke = 2/10).

Memory Consolidation: Background jobs merge multiple episodic events into one semantic fact (e.g., 5 episodic Python questions become 1 semantic memory: "User is learning Python").

Intelligent Forgetting: Uses an Ebbinghaus-inspired decay curve: Decay Score = Importance × e^(-λ × days_since_access). If the score drops too low, the memory is pruned.

Hybrid Retrieval: When a user asks a question, the agent doesn't just do vector search. It combines:

Semantic Similarity (60%)

Recency of creation (20%)

Importance Score (20%)

Real-time Memory Graph: Beautiful UI to visualize how memories are linked.

💻 Tech Stack
Backend: FastAPI, Uvicorn, Python 3.10+

Database: SQLite, SQLAlchemy (ORM)

Vector Store: ChromaDB (Persistent Client)

AI Integration: Google Gemini API (gemini-1.5-flash, text-embedding-004)

Background Jobs: APScheduler

Frontend: React, Vite, TailwindCSS, Framer Motion

Charts: Recharts

Icons: Lucide-React

🗄️ Database Schema (Core Tables)
agents: Stores AI agent profiles.

conversations & messages: Chat history.

memories: The core table. Columns: id, type (episodic/semantic/etc.), content, importance_score, decay_score, last_accessed, is_pinned.

memory_links: Graph relations between memories (e.g., Memory A caused Memory B).

embeddings: Maps SQLite memory_id to ChromaDB vector_id.

consolidation_logs: Tracks when episodic memories were merged.

🔌 API Endpoints (Overview)
Method	Endpoint	Description
POST	/api/v1/chat	Send message. Extracts memories & returns AI response.
GET	/api/v1/memories	List all memories (with filters: type, score).
GET	/api/v1/memories/{id}	Get specific memory details.
DELETE	/api/v1/memories/{id}	Manually delete a memory.
POST	/api/v1/consolidate	Trigger the consolidation engine manually.
POST	/api/v1/decay/trigger	Trigger the forgetting/decay engine.
GET	/api/v1/analytics	Get dashboard stats (memory types, decay graph).
🔄 Memory Lifecycle (How it works)
Encoding: User sends a message.

Working Memory: Message is temporarily buffered.

Extraction: LLM extracts facts and classifies them.

Storage: Facts go to SQLite; Embeddings go to ChromaDB.

Consolidation (Background): Episodic memories are summarized into Semantic memories.

Retrieval: During chat, Hybrid Search finds the top 5 relevant memories.

Decay (Background): Forgetting curve removes low-importance, old memories.

🚀 Installation & Setup
1. Clone the Repository
bash
git clone https://github.com/vishakha2121/agme-memory-engine.git
cd agme-memory-engine
2. Backend Setup
bash
cd backend
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: .\venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
Create a .env file in the backend/ folder and add your API key:

env
GEMINI_API_KEY=your_api_key_here
DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///./data/agme.db
CHROMA_DB_PATH=./data/chroma
Initialize Database & Run Server:

bash
python scripts/init_db.py
uvicorn main:app --reload
(API docs will be available at http://localhost:8000/docs)

3. Frontend Setup
bash
cd ../frontend
npm install
npm run dev
(UI will be available at http://localhost:5173)

📂 Project Folder Structure
text
agme-memory-engine/
├── backend/
│   ├── app/
│   │   ├── api/v1/endpoints/    # Chat, Memory, Analytics routes
│   │   ├── core/                # Config, logging
│   │   ├── db/                  # SQLAlchemy setup
│   │   ├── memory_engine/       # ⭐ Core Logic (Consolidation, Decay)
│   │   ├── models/              # ORM Tables
│   │   ├── schemas/             # Pydantic models
│   │   ├── services/            # Gemini API, Embeddings
│   │   ├── vector_store/        # ChromaDB setup
│   │   └── workers/             # APScheduler background jobs
│   ├── data/                    # SQLite DB & Chroma files
│   ├── scripts/                 # DB init scripts
│   └── main.py                  # Entry point
├── frontend/
│   ├── src/
│   │   ├── components/          # UI components, Chat, Memory Cards
│   │   ├── pages/               # Dashboard, Chat, Memories
│   │   ├── hooks/               # Custom React hooks
│   │   ├── api/                 # Axios API calls
│   │   └── utils/               # Helpers
└── docs/                        # Architecture diagrams
🎬 Demo Flow (What to show in the interview)
Open Chat: Type "Hi, I'm Rahul. I love biryani and I am learning Python."

Watch Memory Creation: The side panel shows 3 new memories being created (Name, Food preference, Learning goal).

Consolidation: Go to the Dashboard and click "Run Consolidation". Watch episodic events merge into semantic facts.

Forgetting: In the Forgetting panel, trigger the Decay job. See how low-importance memories get pruned while high-importance ones survive.

Recall: Refresh the page, start a new chat, and ask "What's my name and what am I learning?" The agent answers perfectly using its Semantic Memory.

🎓 Interview Preparation (Design Decisions)
Q: Why 4 memory types and not a single vector database?

A flat vector database is just a massive bucket of text. Human memory is structured. By separating Episodic (events) from Semantic (facts), we allow the system to consolidate raw events into abstract knowledge over time.

Q: How does the Forgetting Algorithm work?

We use an exponential decay formula: importance * e^(-decay_rate * days_since_access). If a memory is never recalled and its importance is low, its decay score drops below a threshold and it gets pruned. This prevents memory bloat.

Q: Why FastAPI and not Node.js or Django?

FastAPI provides native async support, which is crucial for calling external LLM APIs without blocking the server. Its integration with Pydantic ensures strict data validation, which is essential when parsing unpredictable LLM outputs.

Q: Why is this CPU friendly?

We don't run any LLM weights locally. Text generation and embeddings are handled by Gemini Cloud API. Local computation is limited to SQLite queries and small vector searches on ChromaDB, which easily run on a standard CPU.

🔮 Future Scope
□ Multi-agent shared memory (Agents can read each other's memories).
□ LLM-based Memory Compression to save storage.
□ Local LLM support (Ollama) for offline usage.
□ User feedback loop for retrieval ranking (Reinforcement Learning).
